From 714a8464ab7811244b06ca43ae5cb8589bfe20f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kartheek Palla <palla.kartheekreddy@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 12:59:47 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] feat(oid4vc-service): acquire Authority tokens, and renew
 them before they expire

The Authority claim source read a bearer token from AUTHORITY_TOKEN and sent it
forever. A token in an environment variable cannot outlive its own expiry, so a
deployment configured on Monday stops issuing on Monday plus the token lifetime.
It fails as a 401 from the Authority Service, which reads like a permissions
problem rather than an expiry: nothing restarts, nothing alarms, and issuance is
simply gone.

The issuing service now holds CREDENTIALS rather than a token, and exchanges them
for a fresh one whenever the current token nears expiry.

  - Renewal happens early, by ten seconds or a third of a short lifetime,
    because a token used at the instant it expires is refused by the time it
    arrives and two containers' clocks are not identical.
  - Providers are shared per (token endpoint, client). ClaimSourceFactory builds
    a claim source PER REQUEST, so a provider held as an instance field is an
    empty cache every time: the cache would never once be read and every
    issuance would mint fresh tokens. Keyed rather than global, so two officers
    keep two tokens.
  - Concurrent callers share one exchange. A wallet collecting two credentials
    would otherwise turn a single expiry into a burst of identical requests.
  - A 401 or 403 from the Authority discards the token and retries once, so a
    revocation or a clock disagreement beyond the margin is a retry rather than
    a failed issuance for a holder who is standing there waiting.
  - A partly configured client is refused at construction. Falling back to an
    anonymous call would fail at the Authority on every issuance and point an
    operator at permissions instead of at the missing secret.

AUTHORITY_TOKEN is still honoured when no client credentials are set, for a
deployment whose Authority is fronted by something other than OAuth2.
